<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of the Obelisks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 2nd millennium BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">Obelisks</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> sanctuary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, named after the many standing stones erected in its courtyard. Hundreds of offerings were found buried there, including bronze figurines of gods, some covered in gold leaf, weapons and jewellery. The archaeologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-dunand">Maurice Dunand</a> moved the temple to a new site to excavate the older temple beneath it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-obelisks.jpg" alt="Temple of the Obelisks" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jerzy Strzelecki · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byblos(js)_6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
